                    <div class="rounded-2xl bg-slate-900 text-slate-200 p-6 md:p-7 border">
                        <div class="text-[13px] font-bold tracking-widest text-emerald-400">TL;DR</div>
                        <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] !text-slate-100 leading-7">Under CROA, it is illegal to charge you before work is done. Legit companies never promise a specific score increase, never tell you to lie, and never sell you a CPN. You can do anything a credit repair company can do for free.</p>
                        <div class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-3 gap-2 text-[12px]"><span class="px-3 py-2 rounded-full bg-white/10">No upfront fees</span><span class="px-3 py-2 rounded-full bg-white/10">No 100-point guarantees</span><span class="px-3 py-2 rounded-full bg-white/10">No CPN / new identity</span></div>
                    </div>

                    <h2>Why credit repair scams target beginners</h2>
                    <p>American consumers lost over $20 billion to credit repair scams in recent years according to FTC complaint data. Scammers know that when you're starting at 0, 520, or dealing with collections, you'll pay for hope. They advertise on TikTok, Instagram, and Google with phrases like "Delete bankruptcies in 30 days!" or "We have a loophole to erase bad credit."</p>
                    <p>There is no loophole. Under the Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA), accurate negative information can stay for up to 7 years — bankruptcy 10 years. If it's accurate, no one can legally remove it. What they can do is dispute inaccurate information — which you can do for free.</p>

                    <h2>The 5 Red Flags the CFPB Warns About</h2>
                    <p>The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB) publishes a clear checklist. If you see any of these, walk away and report the company at <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/complaint/" class="underline font-semibold">consumerfinance.gov/complaint</a>:</p>

                    <div class="not-prose my-8 grid gap-4">
                        <div class="rounded-[18px] border-2 border-red-200 bg-red-50/60 p-5 flex gap-4">
                            <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-red-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center font-bold shrink-0">1</div>
                            <div>
                                <h3 class="!mt-0 !mb-1 text-[16px] font-extrabold">Demands payment BEFORE any work is done — Illegal upfront fees</h3>
                                <p class="text-[14px] leading-6 text-slate-700">The Credit Repair Organizations Act makes this illegal. Companies cannot charge before they have performed the promised services and given you a written report. Red flag phrases: "Enrollment fee $499", "First work fee", "Setup fee due today", monthly fee before first dispute results. Legit fee structure: pay only after work, with clear contract and 3-day cancellation right.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rounded-[18px] border-2 border-red-200 bg-red-50/60 p-5 flex gap-4">
                            <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-red-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center font-bold shrink-0">2</div>
                            <div>
                                <h3 class="!mt-0 !mb-1 text-[16px] font-extrabold">Guarantees a specific score increase or promises to delete accurate info</h3>
                                <p class="text-[14px] leading-6 text-slate-700">No one can guarantee "We will raise you 200 points" or "We delete all collections". Experian and Equifax state that only inaccurate, unverified information can be removed. If a company says they can remove a correct late payment, charge-off, or bankruptcy early via "secret methods", it's lying. Vague success rates like "98% deletions" without proof are also a flag.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rounded-[18px] border-2 border-red-200 bg-red-50/60 p-5 flex gap-4">
                            <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-red-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center font-bold shrink-0">3</div>
                            <div>
                                <h3 class="!mt-0 !mb-1 text-[16px] font-extrabold">Tells you to avoid contact with credit bureaus or lie on applications</h3>
                                <p class="text-[14px] leading-6 text-slate-700">CFPB: You have the right to dispute directly with bureaus. Any company that says "Don't talk to Experian, Equifax, TransUnion yourself" or advises you to dispute everything regardless of accuracy is violating CROA. Worse: telling you to provide false info on credit applications is federal fraud.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rounded-[18px] border-2 border-red-200 bg-red-50/60 p-5 flex gap-4">
                            <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-red-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center font-bold shrink-0">4</div>
                            <div>
                                <h3 class="!mt-0 !mb-1 text-[16px] font-extrabold">Sells you a CPN — Credit Privacy Number — or suggests a new identity</h3>
                                <p class="text-[14px] leading-6 text-slate-700">This is the most dangerous scam. CPNs are often stolen Social Security Numbers (SSNs) of children or purchased illegally. Using a CPN to apply for credit is identity fraud, can lead to federal charges. CFPB & FTC: A CPN is not a legal way to hide bad credit or create a new file. Real names for this scam: "credit privacy number", "SCN", "CPN package $899". If offered, report immediately.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rounded-[18px] border-2 border-red-200 bg-red-50/60 p-5 flex gap-4">
                            <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-red-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center font-bold shrink-0">5</div>
                            <div>
                                <h3 class="!mt-0 !mb-1 text-[16px] font-extrabold">No written contract, no clear rights, pushes you to sign fast</h3>
                                <p class="text-[14px] leading-6 text-slate-700">Legally they must give you: a written contract stating total cost, what services, how long, guarantee of 3 business days to cancel with no penalty, and copy of your Consumer Credit File Rights. If there's no contract, pressure tactics ("Offer ends tonight"), or they won't give you your rights disclosure — it's not compliant.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h2>What legitimate credit repair CAN actually do</h2>
                    <p>There are legitimate nonprofit and for-profit credit counseling organizations. But their scope is limited by law:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><b>Review your reports with you</b> — Pull all three bureaus, identify inaccurate, incomplete, or outdated items.</li>
                        <li><b>Help you file disputes for free</b> — Draft dispute letters for errors: wrong balance, account not yours, incorrect late date, duplicate collections. You can file at AnnualCreditReport.com dispute portals.</li>
                        <li><b>Negotiate goodwill or pay-for-delete (no guarantee)</b> — Some creditors will update goodwill adjustments, but they are not required to delete accurate info.</li>
                        <li><b>Build positive history going forward</b> — Set up on-time payment systems, secured cards, credit-builder loans like Self or Chime Credit Builder. This is where real score growth comes — Experian data shows 35% of score is payment history.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>Legit companies: Must be transparent about pricing after service, have physical address, won't ask you to waive your rights, and will encourage you to contact bureaus yourself.</p>

                    <h2>How to repair credit yourself for free — 4 steps</h2>
                    <h3>1. Get free reports weekly</h3>
                    <p>Every US consumer can get reports free weekly at <b>AnnualCreditReport.com</b> — the only official site. Don't use imposter .com sites. Review every account for errors. Equifax reports up to 81 months of history.</p>
                    <h3>2. Dispute errors directly (15-min letters)</h3>
                    <p>For each error: write to bureau (Experian, Equifax, TransUnion) with proof — bank statement, payment record. Bureaus have 30 days to verify. According to Equifax, if creditor doesn't verify, item must be corrected or deleted.</p>
                    <h3>3. Get legitimate nonprofit counseling</h3>
                    <p>NFCC members — National Foundation for Credit Counseling — at nfcc.org offer free or $25 low-fee counseling. NFCC certified agencies exist in all 50 states and must be 501(c)(3) nonprofits. They can set up debt management plans without scams. Avoid any company not listed on NFCC or Department of Justice trustee list.</p>
                    <h3>4. Add positive trade lines</h3>
                    <p>If you have thin file: secured card, report rent with services that report to bureaus, and consider credit-builder products. On-time payments for 6 months can move thin files significantly — this is more powerful than deleting old collections.</p>

                    <div class="not-prose mt-8 rounded-2xl bg-emerald-50 border border-emerald-200 p-6">
                        <div class="font-bold text-emerald-900">ScoreUp Checklist Before Signing Anything</div>
                        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-[14px] text-emerald-900/80">
                            <li>☐ Did I get a written contract with cancellation rights?</li>
                            <li>☐ Are they charging only AFTER work?</li>
                            <li>☐ Did they give me my Credit File Rights notice?</li>
                            <li>☐ Do they avoid score guarantees and CPN talk?</li>
                            <li>☐ Is their address real and listed on NFCC/DOJ if nonprofit?</li>
                            <li>☐ Have I checked CFPB complaint database and BBB?</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <h2>Sources & US Legal Context</h2>
                    <p>All US consumers are protected by federal law regardless of state. Primary sources:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><b>CFPB:</b> Consumer Financial Protection Bureau — credit repair and credit privacy number warnings — consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/</li>
                        <li><b>FTC — Credit Repair Organizations Act (CROA):</b> 15 U.S.C. § 1679 — bans advance fees, requires disclosures</li>
                        <li><b>Experian:</b> How to dispute information on your credit report — experian.com/disputes/</li>
                        <li><b>Equifax:</b> Credit dispute information — equifax.com/personal/disputes/</li>
                        <li><b>AnnualCreditReport.com:</b> Official free weekly reports — by Federal law</li>
                        <li><b>NFCC:</b> nfcc.org — find certified nonprofit counselors</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="text-[12px] text-slate-500">ScoreUp serves US consumers. FICO models, bureau dispute timelines, and CROA rights referenced are United States-specific. This article does not request SSN, bank login, or payment. We never sell CPNs.</p>

                    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
                    <div class="not-prose space-y-3">
                        <details class="group rounded-xl border bg-white p-4 open:shadow-sm"><summary class="font-semibold text-[14px] flex justify-between cursor-pointer list-none">Can a credit repair company remove accurate late payments? <span class="group-open:rotate-45 transition">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-[14px] leading-6 text-slate-600">No. If the late payment is accurate and verified by the creditor, it must stay for up to 7 years per FCRA. A legitimate company can only help you dispute if the date, balance, or ownership is wrong. Paying them to "delete accurate info" is a scam signal.</p></details>
                        <details class="group rounded-xl border bg-white p-4 open:shadow-sm"><summary class="font-semibold text-[14px] flex justify-between cursor-pointer list-none">Is paying a monthly fee for credit repair legal? <span class="group-open:rotate-45 transition">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-[14px] leading-6 text-slate-600">Only after service is performed. Under CROA, monthly fees before any work is charged is illegal. A compliant company might charge monthly after it sends initial disputes and shows you results, with clear contract. Upfront "work fees" on day one are not allowed.</p></details>
                        <details class="group rounded-xl border bg-white p-4 open:shadow-sm"><summary class="font-semibold text-[14px] flex justify-between cursor-pointer list-none">Are CPNs ever legal? <span class="group-open:rotate-45 transition">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-[14px] leading-6 text-slate-600">No. CPNs are marketed as a new 9-digit number to use instead of SSN. In reality they are often stolen SSNs. Using a CPN on a credit application is identity fraud under federal law. CFPB and Social Security Administration warn never to use one.</p></details>
                        <details class="group rounded-xl border bg-white p-4 open:shadow-sm"><summary class="font-semibold text-[14px] flex justify-between cursor-pointer list-none">What's a free alternative to credit repair companies? <span class="group-open:rotate-45 transition">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-[14px] leading-6 text-slate-600">AnnualCreditReport.com for reports, bureau dispute portals for free disputes, NFCC nonprofit counselors for free budgeting plans, and self-building with secured cards and Self/Chime Credit Builder. All have no SSN sharing beyond normal applications.</p></details>
                        <details class="group rounded-xl border bg-white p-4 open:shadow-sm"><summary class="font-semibold text-[14px] flex justify-between cursor-pointer list-none">How do I report a credit repair scam? <span class="group-open:rotate-45 transition">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-[14px] leading-6 text-slate-600">File at FTC ReportFraud.ftc.gov, CFPB complaint portal (consumerfinance.gov/complaint), and your state Attorney General. Keep contracts, receipts, ads, and communications.</p></details>
                    </div>

